# Permissions per table.
#
# A role says what verbs a user may use; a grant says which tables they may use
# them on. The rule is one sentence: **a user's grants, if they have any, are the
# whole story, and a user with none is governed by their role.**
#
# A corpus file is one anonymous session, the same as `users.bgvql`, so what
# these cases pin is the shape of the statements and the refusals reachable
# before the first `DEFINE USER` closes the store. What a grant *does* to a
# signed-in session is asserted where a session can sign in —
# `crates/bgv-db-session/tests/grants.rs` — for the same reason the per-role
# checks live there.

--- case: a namespace, a database and two tables to work in
DEFINE NAMESPACE prod;
USE NAMESPACE prod;
DEFINE DATABASE shop;
USE DATABASE shop;
DEFINE TABLE users;
DEFINE TABLE orders;
--- expect: ok

--- case: a grant names a user, and there is none yet
GRANT read ON users TO nobody;
--- expect: error Unknown

--- case: a grant names a table, and this is not one
GRANT read ON nonesuch TO nobody;
--- expect: error Unknown

--- case: a grant names a verb the language has
GRANT scribble ON users TO nobody;
--- expect: error NoSuchVerb

--- case: and so does a revocation
REVOKE scribble ON users FROM nobody;
--- expect: error NoSuchVerb

--- case: a user, which closes the store
DEFINE USER ada ON prod.shop ROLE editor PASSWORD 'correct horse';
--- expect: ok

--- case: granting is administering, so an anonymous session may not
GRANT read ON users TO ada;
--- expect: error NotSignedIn

--- case: and neither may it revoke
REVOKE read ON users FROM ada;
--- expect: error NotSignedIn
